2026-06-30 15:33:50,877 - openai._base_client - DEBUG - Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/chat/completions', 'timeout': 300, 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-66665f13-284a-48d2-81a5-f47709f216d3', 'security': {'bearer_auth': True}, 'content': None, 'json_data': {'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are an expert proteomics assistant. Return valid JSON only.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'You are a proteomics expert analyzing crosslinking mass spectrometry (XL-MS) datasets.\n\nYour task is to cluster raw spectral files into biologically meaningful groups.\n\nA cluster should represent a unique combination of:\n1. Biological / experimental condition\n   - Examples: treatment, control, carbon source, growth medium, cell state, tissue, disease state, time point, perturbation, purified complex, recombinant standard set.\n2. Source organism of the proteome being analyzed\n   - Return the full scientific name.\n   - Use the organism of the biological sample or proteome of interest, not necessarily the expression host.\n   - Example: if human proteins are expressed in E. coli and analyzed as human recombinant standards, the source organism is Homo sapiens.\n   - If the dataset intentionally contains multiple source organisms, create separate clusters by organism when possible.\n3. Crosslinking reagent\n   - Examples: BS3, DSS, DSSO, DSBU, formaldehyde.\n   - If multiple crosslinkers are used, create separate clusters by reagent when possible.\n\nDo NOT split clusters by:\n- fraction number\n- LC fraction\n- technical replicate\n- biological replicate ID\n- injection number\n- file numbering\n- enrichment fraction\n- instrument run order\n- search batch\n- calibration/QC files\n\nReplicates, fractions, and repeated injections from the same biological condition should be grouped together.\n\n---\n\nINPUTS\n\nRaw filenames:\nCT1.raw\nCT2.raw\nCT3.raw\nCT4.raw\nCT5.raw\nSHH1.raw\nSHH2.raw\nSHH3.raw\nSHH4.raw\nSHH5.raw\n\nPublication text and PRIDE metadata:\n[TITLE]\nSecretome profile of living constructs assembled from cell-to-cell crosslinking of human adipose tissue-derived mesenchymal stromal cells\n\n[ABSTRACT]\nWe report proteomic data from living constructs assembled entirely from human cells covalently tethered via interfacial bridges with a glycosaminoglycan. Here, bioorthogonal azide groups are installed in cell surface glycoproteins via metabolic glycoengineering, allowing for catalyst-free click-chemistry coupling with a complementary hyaluronan biopolymer. This cell-to-cell tethering strategy enables the use of human cells as active crosslinkers and main building blocks of macroscale, tissue-dense living materials. In light of this, we explore the secretome profile of these constructs following a maturation period of 3 days. Bioinformatic analysis and matrisome annotation revealed the presence of a wide range of proteins related to extracellular matrix organization, assembly and remodeling, as well as proteins involved in cell signaling and migration. These findings highlight the potential of this cell-to-cell tethering strategy for creating nascent living tissue mimetics that can be explored in tissue engineering and regenerative medicine endeavors.\n\n[METHODS]\nSample harvesting Upon a 3-day maturation period, the secretome of disc-shaped living materials comprised by human adipose tissue-derived mesenchymal stromal cells (Ø = 6 mm × 1.5 mm height, n = 5) was evaluated. Briefly, following maturation, these constructs were washed three times in serum-free culture medium (αMEM, 4 mL t = 5 min), before adding serum-free culture medium (2 mL) and returning to cell incubator (24 h) for secretome accumulation. Afterwards, medium was collected, centrifuged (1500 g, 4 ºC) to remove cell debris and placed on ice until analyzed.  LC-MS/MS analysis Samples were processed for proteomic analysis following the single-pot, solid-phase-enhanced sample-preparation (SP3) procedure and enzymatically digested with Trypsin/LysC. Then, samples were analyzed using a nanoLC-MS/MS system composed of a Q Exactive Hybrid Quadrupole-Orbitrap mass spectrometer (Thermo Scientific) coupled to an UltiMate 3000 liquid chromatography system (Dionex). Peptides (500 ng) from each sample were loaded onto a trap cartridge (Acclaim PepMap C18, 100 Å, 5 mm × 0.3 mm, Thermo Scientific) in a mobile phase of 2 % acetonitrile, 0.1 % formic acid at 10 μL/min. After 3 min loading, the trap column was switched in-line to an EASY- Spray column (PepMap RSLC, C18, 2 μm, 50 cm × 75 μm inner diameter, Thermo Scientific) at a flow rate of 250 nL/min. Separation was achieved by mixing mobile phase A (0.1 % formic acid) and B (80 % acetonitrile, 0.1 % formic acid) with the following gradient: 5 min (2.5 to 10 % B), 120 min (10 to 30 % B), 20 min (30 to 50 % B), 5 min (50 to 99 % B), and 10 min (hold 99 % B). Afterwards, the column was equilibrated with solvent B (2.5 %, 17 min). The mass spectrometer was controlled by Xcalibur 4.0 and Tune 2.11 software (Thermo Scientific), operated at 1.9 kV spray voltage in data-dependent positive acquisition mode over a 380-1580 m/z range, alternating with HCD MS/MS of the 10 most intense peaks (normalized collision energy of 27 %). Global settings were as follows: ‘use lock masses’ option set to ‘best’ (m/z 445.12003), ‘lock mass injection’ performed in ‘full MS’ mode, chromatographic peak width set at 15 s. Full scan parameters were set to a resolution of 70 000 (m/z 200), automatic gain control target of 3 × 106 and maximum injection time of 120 ms. Data-dependent settings were as follows: minimum automatic gain control target of 8 × 103; intensity threshold set at 7.3 × 104; ‘Charge exclusion’ set to encompass ‘unassigned’, ‘1’,  ‘8’, and ‘>  8’; ‘Peptide match’ set to ‘preferred’, ‘Exclude isotopes’ set to ‘on’, ‘Dynamic exclusion’ set to ‘45 s’. MS2 settings included: Microscans set to 1, resolution at 35 000 (m/z 200), automatic gain control target of 2 × 105, maximum injection time of 110 ms, isolation window of 2.0 m/z, dynamic first mass and ‘Spectrum data type’ set to ‘Profile’.\n\n\nPreviously extracted organism(s) and NCBI taxid(s):\nHomo sapiens (human)\n\n---\n\nTASKS\n\n1. Identify the distinct biological/experimental conditions described by the publication, PRIDE metadata, and filenames.\n\n2. Assign every raw file to exactly one cluster.\n\n3. Define clusters using the smallest number of biologically meaningful groups. Do not over-cluster based only on replicate, fraction, or run-number differences.\n\n4. For each cluster, report:\n   - cluster_id: "cluster_1", "cluster_2", etc.\n   - cluster_label: short machine-readable label using lowercase words separated by underscores\n   - description: concise human-readable summary of the condition\n   - organism: full scientific name of the proteome/sample organism\n   - taxid: NCBI Taxonomy CURIE, e.g. "NCBITaxon:9606"; use null if unknown\n   - crosslinker: crosslinking reagent; use null if unknown\n   - biological_condition: short phrase describing the treatment/control/state\n   - files: list of raw filenames assigned to this cluster\n   - confidence: "high", "medium", or "low"\n   - evidence_quote: exact sentence or short phrase from the publication, PRIDE metadata, or filename pattern that supports this cluster assignment\n   - reasoning: brief explanation of why these files belong together\n\n5. Assign files that cannot be confidently interpreted to cluster_0 with:\n   - cluster_label: "unassigned"\n   - confidence: "low"\n   - evidence_quote: null\n   - reasoning: explain why assignment was not possible\n\n6. QC, calibrant, blank, wash, or test files should be assigned to cluster_0 unless the publication clearly states they are part of the biological experiment.\n\n7. If all biological files appear to come from one condition, create only cluster_1 plus cluster_0 if there are unassigned/QC files.\n\n8. Confidence guidelines:\n   - "high": filenames and publication/metadata agree clearly\n   - "medium": assignment is likely but partially inferred from filenames or incomplete metadata\n   - "low": weak evidence, ambiguous naming, missing metadata, or conflicting information\n\n9. If publication text and filenames conflict, prioritize the publication/PRIDE metadata, but mention the conflict in reasoning.\n\n10. Return ONLY valid JSON. Do not include markdown, comments, or explanatory text outside the JSON.\n\n---\n\nOUTPUT JSON STRUCTURE\n\n{\n  "clusters": [\n    {\n      "cluster_id": "cluster_1",\n      "cluster_label": "yeast_glucose_bs3",\n      "description": "Saccharomyces cerevisiae samples grown in glucose-rich medium and crosslinked with BS3.",\n      "organism": "Saccharomyces cerevisiae",\n      "taxid": "NCBITaxon:4932",\n      "crosslinker": "BS3",\n      "biological_condition": "glucose-rich medium",\n      "files": [\n        "YeastGlu_R1.raw",\n        "YeastGlu_R2.raw",\n        "YeastGlu_fraction_01.raw"\n      ],\n      "confidence": "high",\n      "evidence_quote": "Yeast cells were cultured in glucose- or glycerol-containing medium and crosslinked with BS3.",\n      "reasoning": "The filenames contain Glu and the publication states that glucose-grown yeast samples were crosslinked with BS3."\n    },\n    {\n      "cluster_id": "cluster_0",\n      "cluster_label": "unassigned",\n      "description": "Files that could not be confidently assigned to a biological XL-MS condition.",\n      "organism": null,\n      "taxid": null,\n      "crosslinker": null,\n      "biological_condition": null,\n      "files": [\n        "calibrant.raw",\n        "QC.raw"\n      ],\n      "confidence": "low",\n      "evidence_quote": null,\n      "reasoning": "These files appear to be QC or calibration runs rather than biological samples."\n    }\n  ],\n  "summary": {\n    "n_input_files": 5,\n    "n_assigned_files": 3,\n    "n_unassigned_files": 2,\n    "n_biological_clusters": 1,\n    "overall_confidence": "high"\n  }\n}'}], 'model': 'gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17', 'store': True, 'temperature': 0.3}}
