2026-06-30 15:33:55,304 - openai._base_client - DEBUG - Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/chat/completions', 'timeout': 300, 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-5971fc1d-273f-49e9-b546-f5a9054ff14b', 'security': {'bearer_auth': True}, 'content': None, 'json_data': {'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are an expert SDRF-Proteomics annotator for crosslinking mass spectrometry (XL-MS) datasets.\n\nExtract the following SDRF metadata terms from the provided publication text and/or PRIDE project description.\n\nTERMS TO EXTRACT:\n─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nterm: disease\nsdrf_column: characteristics[disease]\nontology: EFO:0000408, MONDO, DOID, PATO\ndescription: Disease state of the sample.\ninstructions: For healthy/normal samples use "normal" (PATO:0000461). For diseased\n  samples use MONDO or DOID terms. Return null only if completely unstated.\n  Most XL-MS samples are "normal" unless explicitly studying a disease model.\n\nterm: phenotype\nsdrf_column: characteristics[phenotype]\nontology: EFO:0000651, PATO, EFO\ndescription: Observable characteristics or traits (drug sensitivity, molecular markers,\n  expression phenotypes, environmental responses).\ninstructions: Return null if not stated. This captures how the sample behaves/appears\n  (distinct from disease diagnosis).\n\nterm: treatment\nsdrf_column: characteristics[treatment]\nontology: EFO:0000727, NCIT, EFO\ndescription: Treatment or perturbation applied to the sample (drug, stimulus,\n  environmental stress, growth condition change).\ninstructions: Return null if not stated or not applicable. Examples: "doxycycline\n  treatment", "heat shock", "DMSO vehicle control".\n\nterm: compound\nsdrf_column: characteristics[compound]\nontology: CHEBI:24431, CHEBI, NCIT, EFO\ndescription: Chemical compound or drug applied to the sample.\ninstructions: Use ChEBI if possible. Return null if no compound treatment. Do NOT\n  include the crosslinking reagent here — that goes in comment[cross-linker].\n\nterm: dose\nsdrf_column: characteristics[dose]\nontology: NCIT:C25488\ndescription: Dose or concentration of compound treatment.\npattern: number + unit (e.g. 10 mg/kg, 50 uM, 100 nM, 10 mg/mL)\ninstructions: Return null if no compound treatment or dose not stated.\n\nterm: genetic modification\nsdrf_column: characteristics[genetic modification]\nontology: EFO:0000510, EFO\ndescription: Method of genetic modification (knockout, knockdown, overexpression,\n  transduction). Describes HOW the modification was done.\ninstructions: Return null if not stated.\n\nterm: perturbation\nsdrf_column: characteristics[perturbation]\nontology: PRIDE:0000700\ndescription: Type of perturbation applied (environmental/experimental perturbation).\ninstructions: Return null if not stated or not applicable.\n\nterm: depletion\nsdrf_column: characteristics[depletion]\nontology: PRIDE:0000020\ndescription: Whether abundant protein depletion was performed.\nallowed_values: no depletion, depletion, not applicable\ninstructions: Return "no depletion" if not mentioned. Return "depletion" if\n  high-abundance protein removal is described (e.g. albumin depletion).\n─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\nReturn a single JSON object where each key is the exact sdrf_column string shown above.\nFor each key, return a dict with:\n  "value": the extracted value (string, list, or null)\n  "accession": ontology accession if resolvable, else null\n  "confidence": "high", "medium", or "low"\n  "evidence_quote": short verbatim quote from the text, or null\n  "evidence_location": location in the paper (e.g. "Methods"), or null'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '[TITLE]\nSecretome profile of living constructs assembled from cell-to-cell crosslinking of human adipose tissue-derived mesenchymal stromal cells\n\n[ABSTRACT]\nWe report proteomic data from living constructs assembled entirely from human cells covalently tethered via interfacial bridges with a glycosaminoglycan. Here, bioorthogonal azide groups are installed in cell surface glycoproteins via metabolic glycoengineering, allowing for catalyst-free click-chemistry coupling with a complementary hyaluronan biopolymer. This cell-to-cell tethering strategy enables the use of human cells as active crosslinkers and main building blocks of macroscale, tissue-dense living materials. In light of this, we explore the secretome profile of these constructs following a maturation period of 3 days. Bioinformatic analysis and matrisome annotation revealed the presence of a wide range of proteins related to extracellular matrix organization, assembly and remodeling, as well as proteins involved in cell signaling and migration. These findings highlight the potential of this cell-to-cell tethering strategy for creating nascent living tissue mimetics that can be explored in tissue engineering and regenerative medicine endeavors.\n\n[METHODS]\nSample harvesting Upon a 3-day maturation period, the secretome of disc-shaped living materials comprised by human adipose tissue-derived mesenchymal stromal cells (Ø = 6 mm × 1.5 mm height, n = 5) was evaluated. Briefly, following maturation, these constructs were washed three times in serum-free culture medium (αMEM, 4 mL t = 5 min), before adding serum-free culture medium (2 mL) and returning to cell incubator (24 h) for secretome accumulation. Afterwards, medium was collected, centrifuged (1500 g, 4 ºC) to remove cell debris and placed on ice until analyzed.  LC-MS/MS analysis Samples were processed for proteomic analysis following the single-pot, solid-phase-enhanced sample-preparation (SP3) procedure and enzymatically digested with Trypsin/LysC. Then, samples were analyzed using a nanoLC-MS/MS system composed of a Q Exactive Hybrid Quadrupole-Orbitrap mass spectrometer (Thermo Scientific) coupled to an UltiMate 3000 liquid chromatography system (Dionex). Peptides (500 ng) from each sample were loaded onto a trap cartridge (Acclaim PepMap C18, 100 Å, 5 mm × 0.3 mm, Thermo Scientific) in a mobile phase of 2 % acetonitrile, 0.1 % formic acid at 10 μL/min. After 3 min loading, the trap column was switched in-line to an EASY- Spray column (PepMap RSLC, C18, 2 μm, 50 cm × 75 μm inner diameter, Thermo Scientific) at a flow rate of 250 nL/min. Separation was achieved by mixing mobile phase A (0.1 % formic acid) and B (80 % acetonitrile, 0.1 % formic acid) with the following gradient: 5 min (2.5 to 10 % B), 120 min (10 to 30 % B), 20 min (30 to 50 % B), 5 min (50 to 99 % B), and 10 min (hold 99 % B). Afterwards, the column was equilibrated with solvent B (2.5 %, 17 min). The mass spectrometer was controlled by Xcalibur 4.0 and Tune 2.11 software (Thermo Scientific), operated at 1.9 kV spray voltage in data-dependent positive acquisition mode over a 380-1580 m/z range, alternating with HCD MS/MS of the 10 most intense peaks (normalized collision energy of 27 %). Global settings were as follows: ‘use lock masses’ option set to ‘best’ (m/z 445.12003), ‘lock mass injection’ performed in ‘full MS’ mode, chromatographic peak width set at 15 s. Full scan parameters were set to a resolution of 70 000 (m/z 200), automatic gain control target of 3 × 106 and maximum injection time of 120 ms. Data-dependent settings were as follows: minimum automatic gain control target of 8 × 103; intensity threshold set at 7.3 × 104; ‘Charge exclusion’ set to encompass ‘unassigned’, ‘1’,  ‘8’, and ‘>  8’; ‘Peptide match’ set to ‘preferred’, ‘Exclude isotopes’ set to ‘on’, ‘Dynamic exclusion’ set to ‘45 s’. MS2 settings included: Microscans set to 1, resolution at 35 000 (m/z 200), automatic gain control target of 2 × 105, maximum injection time of 110 ms, isolation window of 2.0 m/z, dynamic first mass and ‘Spectrum data type’ set to ‘Profile’.\n'}], 'model': 'gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17', 'store': True, 'temperature': 0.3}}
