umn was switched in-line to an EASY- Spray column (PepMap RSLC, C18, 2 μm, 50 cm × 75 μm inner diameter, Thermo Scientific) at a flow rate of 250 nL/min. Separation was achieved by mixing mobile phase A (0.1 % formic acid) and B (80 % acetonitrile, 0.1 % formic acid) with the following gradient: 5 min (2.5 to 10 % B), 120 min (10 to 30 % B), 20 min (30 to 50 % B), 5 min (50 to 99 % B), and 10 min (hold 99 % B). Afterwards, the column was equilibrated with solvent B (2.5 %, 17 min). The mass spectrometer was controlled by Xcalibur 4.0 and Tune 2.11 software (Thermo Scientific), operated at 1.9 kV spray voltage in data-dependent positive acquisition mode over a 380-1580 m/z range, alternating with HCD MS/MS of the 10 most intense peaks (normalized collision energy of 27 %). Global settings were as follows: ‘use lock masses’ option set to ‘best’ (m/z 445.12003), ‘lock mass injection’ performed in ‘full MS’ mode, chromatographic peak width set at 15 s. Full scan parameters were set to a resolution of 70 000 (m/z 200), automatic gain control target of 3 × 106 and maximum injection time of 120 ms. Data-dependent settings were as follows: minimum automatic gain control target of 8 × 103; intensity threshold set at 7.3 × 104; ‘Charge exclusion’ set to encompass ‘unassigned’, ‘1’,  ‘8’, and ‘>  8’; ‘Peptide match’ set to ‘preferred’, ‘Exclude isotopes’ set to ‘on’, ‘Dynamic exclusion’ set to ‘45 s’. MS2 settings included: Microscans set to 1, resolution at 35 000 (m/z 200), automatic gain control target of 2 × 105, maximum injection time of 110 ms, isolation window of 2.0 m/z, dynamic first mass and ‘Spectrum data type’ set to ‘Profile’.\n'}], 'model': 'gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17', 'store': True, 'temperature': 0.3}}
